Have you ever felt frustrated when one of your "best" characters (definition: a
"best" character is one who died because you made a mistake but *before* it was
time for you to run and get the last bus :-) ) got this cursed -3 dagger welded
into his/her hand (just tried to #force a chest without risking his/her +3 long
sword, hit 'B' instead of 'b' by mistake and got an unknown dagger instead of
good'ole uncursed +0), found the appropriate altar after almost getting killed
twice only to find out that no "potion of water" is available? Argh, there's a
fountain on the level, and I have this stupid potion of paralysis, I could
discard it and get the required water, if only there was a #fill command...

I've thought about the problem and it isn't as easy to solve as I originally
thought. The water is no problem, you would be allowed to #fill whenever you are
sitting on top of a fountain or sink; the water would normally come out
uncursed, with the usual fountain percentage of getting blessed or cursed water
instead (but you wouldn't know it unless you were a priest); you would, of
course, be subjected to the "standard" fountain/sink risk of attracting
something unfriendly while taking the water from the tap. But the big problem is
the container. I can see three possibilities:

1. Start distributing empty bottles (why not, there are wands of nothing after
   all :-) ). I don't like that idea, though, because it would be something
   created on purpose for the #fill command.

2. Change the code so that, whenever you drink a potion, the item in your
   inventory is replaced with "empty bottle". You can then discard it if you
   want, or keep it to fill it with water. Although this is very realistic, it
   means you may need more keystrokes to process potions in case you're not
   interested in the resulting "corpses"; not a problem for me (I'd keep them),
   but what about other players?

3. Require the player to "sacrifice" another potion he already has, discarding
   the contents of the bottle and replacing it with water from the fountain. In
   that case there should be a probability that the player spills some of the
   contents on himself and gets affected by the potion :-)

In any case, this should not become an infinite supply of sanctified water: it
should not be possible to use a given vial more than once for this purpose. The
vial could disintegrate after the water is used (ie potions of water don't leave
any bottle when you use them), and/or it could be marked with the dungeon level
it was filled up in and a mysterious force would prevent you from filling it
again less than 5-10 levels (random) below. This also means that priests can't
keep filling vials from a fountain until they get holy water - well, they
could, but they'd have to go down 5-10 levels every time, chances are that
they'd meet an altar well before :-)

